WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:01.890
MAX SALTONSTALL: Welcome to
"BeyondCorp in a Bottle,"

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:03.556
where we explore
journeys into the world

00:00:03.556 --> 00:00:05.670
of context-aware access
in zero trust networks.

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:08.808
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:12.410
More and more
companies have shifted

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:14.226
to bring your own
device policies,

00:00:14.226 --> 00:00:16.100
allowing employees to
work with the computers

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:17.660
they know and love.

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:21.080
This also reduces the IT spend
per employee, a popular--

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:23.239
if troublesome-- trend.

00:00:23.239 --> 00:00:25.280
But as we think about
securing corporate services

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:27.470
and applications,
we need to ask,

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:29.870
how healthy are those devices?

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:32.479
Should I trust them
with my company's data?

00:00:32.479 --> 00:00:33.710
No.

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:37.920
No, you should not, because
you don't know what's on them.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:39.650
You don't know
anything about them.

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:41.960
Let's look at how our friends
at Slager Bank and Trust

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:44.570
tackle this problem using
endpoint verification

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:47.390
and Access Context
Manager on GCP.

00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:49.652
First, gather the data.

00:00:49.652 --> 00:00:51.110
They used the Google
Admin controls

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:53.060
to install the Endpoint
Verification Chrome

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:56.300
extension on all devices
under active management.

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:58.550
They also told employees
using their own laptops

00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:00.290
to install the same extension.

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:03.260
Next, they turned to GCP's
Access Context Manager

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:06.320
to create rules and
policies around trust.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:07.790
They could define
their own rules

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:11.930
for earning enough trust to
get to internal applications.

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:14.360
To start out, they made rules
based on minimum Chrome OS

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:15.260
version.

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:17.360
That way, nobody with an
out of date Chromebook

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:20.030
would be able to access
their company data.

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:22.130
As they gathered more
device information,

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:24.200
they added rules
based on other factors

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:26.240
to make sure nobody
with an unencrypted disk

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:29.090
could leak information
through a lost device.

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:30.950
In the end, they
settled on access policy

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:32.300
that looked like this--

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:35.390
a low-trust tier for an
up-to-date device and a higher

00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:39.056
trust tier for encrypted
and up-to-date devices.

00:01:39.056 --> 00:01:41.180
When someone signs in who
doesn't meet the security

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:43.700
criteria, they'll
see this instead.

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:45.320
This step allowed
the teams at Slager

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:47.840
to improve their control
over secure access

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:50.300
to the company's systems
while still preserving

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:52.330
bring your own device policies.

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:54.770
To get more detail, check
out my post linked below

00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:57.890
for step-by-step instructions,
and subscribe for more videos

00:01:57.890 --> 00:02:00.020
on context-aware access.

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:03.350
When it comes to protecting your
company, keep your data yours.

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:06.400
[MUSIC PLAYING]

